# Test Strategy (Planning Phase)

Quality decisions made during planning prevent wasted effort writing tests that get flagged in review. This rule captures what to decide upfront — not what to verify after.

> **Companion rules**: `bdd-story-analysis.mdc` covers scenario writing, pyramid distribution, and test level selection. Framework-specific testing rules cover syntax and tooling. This rule fills the gap between those.

## Choose the Right Test Level

When planning which level to test a behavior at, apply the cost principle: test at the lowest level that catches the bug.

| If the logic is... | Plan a... | Not a... |
|---|---|---|
| Pure function (validation, formatting, calculation) | Unit test calling the function directly | Component render, controller test, or integration test |
| Module with dependencies on other internal modules | Integration test with real or stubbed collaborators | E2E test |
| UI component with user interaction | Integration test with a DOM testing library | E2E test |
| Full user journey, multi-service flow, or real external dependency | E2E test (only if critical path) | Integration test with excessive mocking |

Higher-level tests add setup cost, execution time, and failure surface without catching additional bugs when the logic is testable at a lower level.

## Verify Happy Path Coverage First

Before planning error and edge case tests, verify that happy path tests exist for the behavior under change. When a story adds error handling, the plan must confirm:

- At least one test exercises the successful/default flow end-to-end
- The happy path test was not removed or broken by the current changes

**Common planning trap**: A story scoped to "error handling" produces a test file titled "Failure Handling" with zero happy path tests. Error tests without a baseline success test prove the code fails gracefully — but not that it works at all.

If happy path tests don't exist, add them to the plan as the first test task. They establish the baseline that error tests deviate from.

## Plan for Complete Error Coverage

When a feature handles multiple error states (error codes, fallback paths, redirect conditions, exception types), plan tests for **all** of them, not a representative sample.

**Common planning trap**: Noting "test error handling" and listing 3 of 7 error codes. The 4 you skip are the ones with bugs.

During planning, enumerate every error the implementation will handle:
- Every error code the caller or UI responds to
- Every validation rule with a distinct message
- Every conditional redirect, fallback path, or exception filter

Each gets its own planned test case or parameterized test row.

## Plan Assertions on Behavior, Not Internals

When defining what each test should verify, plan to assert on observable outputs. This avoids writing tests that break on refactor.

| Plan to assert on... | Instead of... |
|---|---|
| Response status codes and bodies | Internal method call counts |
| Rendered text, state, or navigation | Internal function invocations |
| User-visible or caller-visible error messages | Specific internal exception types |
| Return values and output state | Private field values |
| Database state after operations (integration) | Repository method call counts |

**Planning question for each test**: "If someone refactored the internals without changing what the caller/user sees, would this test still pass?" If no, rethink the assertion.

## Plan Edge Cases by Category

For each behavior in the plan, scan these categories and note which apply. Not every category applies to every feature — only include relevant ones.

| Category | What to plan for |
|---|---|
| **Boundaries** | n-1, n, n+1 for every limit in the feature |
| **Empty/zero/null** | Zero items, empty strings, missing params, null values |
| **State** | Mid-operation failures, stale data, concurrent access |
| **Interruptions** | Network loss, timeout, session expiry during action |
| **Conditional branches** | Both sides of `||`/`&&`/`??`, each step in fallback chains (`a ?? b ?? c ?? default`), null at each level of optional chains (`a?.b?.c`) |
| **Context variation** | Non-default values for contextual dependencies: route path, locale, user role, tenant, environment, feature flags |

Reference `edge-case-heuristics.mdc` for detailed checklists when the feature touches data inputs, CRUD operations, or web navigation.

## Avoid Planning Wasteful Tests

These anti-patterns are cheaper to prevent during planning than to flag during review.

| Anti-pattern | Planning fix |
|---|---|
| **Tautological** — test always passes (`expect(true).toBe(true)`, `Assert.NotNull(result)`, `assert result is not None`) | Plan a specific expected value for every assertion |
| **Mirror** — test re-implements production logic to derive expected value | Plan expected values as hardcoded literals from the spec |
| **Framework** — tests that a library works, not that your code works | Only plan tests for YOUR logic; assume the framework does its job |
| **Overlap** — same behavior tested at multiple pyramid levels | Assign each behavior to ONE level during planning; only duplicate for high-risk paths |
| **Wrong-level** — pure logic tested through a full integration setup | See "Choose the Right Test Level" above |

## Planning Checklist

Before finalizing a test plan, verify:

- [ ] Happy path tests exist (or are planned) for every route/controller/handler/service under change
- [ ] Each acceptance criterion has a test at a specific pyramid level
- [ ] Error paths are enumerated individually, not grouped as "test error handling"
- [ ] Compound conditions and fallback chains have each branch tested independently
- [ ] Planned assertions target observable behavior, not internals
- [ ] Relevant edge case categories are addressed
- [ ] No test is planned at a higher level than necessary
- [ ] Each planned test verifies one behavior (name describes what it tests)
